1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,985
Which place on Earth welcomes the New Year
first... and which one is last?

2
00:00:05,702 --> 00:00:07,900
Our planet has twenty-four main time

3
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:11,153
zones, but their borders zigzag around
countries.

4
00:00:11,730 --> 00:00:13,638
And in the middle of the Pacific runs

5
00:00:13,658 --> 00:00:17,274
the International Date Line, where every
new day begins.

6
00:00:18,015 --> 00:00:21,283
The first country to hit midnight is
Kiribati,

7
00:00:21,303 --> 00:00:25,174
about one hundred twenty thousand people
on thirty-three islands.

8
00:00:25,821 --> 00:00:29,089
On Kiritimati Island, villages called
London, Banana

9
00:00:29,109 --> 00:00:32,225
and Poland celebrate before anyone else on
Earth.

10
00:00:33,046 --> 00:00:37,139
Until nineteen ninety-five, the line cut
Kiribati in half, so

11
00:00:37,159 --> 00:00:40,519
its capital and its eastern islands lived
a whole day apart.

12
00:00:41,062 --> 00:00:43,853
So Kiribati simply moved the line, and

13
00:00:43,873 --> 00:00:46,246
that's why it bends thousands of
kilometers east.

14
00:00:47,009 --> 00:00:51,682
Then midnight sweeps west: New Zealand,
Australia,

15
00:00:51,702 --> 00:00:57,142
Asia, Europe, Africa, and finally the
Americas.

16
00:00:57,955 --> 00:01:01,827
One of the last inhabited places to
celebrate is American Samoa.

17
00:01:02,323 --> 00:01:05,393
Just about seventy kilometers away is
Samoa, on the

18
00:01:05,413 --> 00:01:07,704
other side of the line, a full day ahead.

19
00:01:08,223 --> 00:01:13,640
Celebrate in Samoa, fly across the line,
and you can welcome the New Year twice.

20
00:01:14,125 --> 00:01:16,161
But the very last spots on the map

21
00:01:16,181 --> 00:01:19,391
are Baker and Howland Islands, where
nobody lives.

22
00:01:19,968 --> 00:01:24,060
So the final New Year's party on Earth
belongs to... seabirds.
